$ gragas init --help
--- stdout

  Sets up the folder you are in. In a
  folder that is neither a
  project nor a rulebook it asks which.
  A rulebook gets its
  identity, its structure and a first
  history entry, and the
  two lines that wire the guard are
  printed for you to add.
  The project form is not built yet.

  Without --apply it prints the plan and
  writes nothing.

    gragas init --kind rulebook --name
  my-rules --owner me
    gragas init --kind rulebook --name
  my-rules --owner me --apply

  USAGE


    gragas init [--flags]


  FLAGS

    --accessible
     Plain questions (ACCESSIBLE)
    --apply
     Write the files
    --config
     The settings file (GRAGAS_CONFIG)
    --docs-url
     The base URL of the published pages
    -h --help
     Help for init
    --import
     A part taken from another rulebook,
     RULEBOOK@VERSION=PART,PART=sha256:<hash>
     (repeat for more)
    --json
     Answer in one JSON document
    --kind
     What the folder is: project or
     rulebook
    --language
     The authoritative language (en)
    --layer
     The slug of the first layer (core)
    --licence
     The licence of the rules
     (CC-BY-4.0)
    --name
     The rulebook's short name, a slug
    --no-color
     No colour (NO_COLOR)
    --owner
     Who owns the rulebook, shown in its
     views; it grants nothing (repeat
     for more)
    --parent
     An overlay's parent rulebook
    --parent-hash
     The content hash of the parent's
     pinned release, sha256:<64 hex
     digits>
    --parent-version
     The parent's version the overlay
     pins
    --part
     The slug of the first part (core)
    --plain
     Print plainly: nothing moves
    --presets-licence
     The licence of the presets (MIT)
    --rulebook-kind
     Base, overlay, companion or fork
     (base)
    --version
     The starting version the first
     release publishes (1.0.0)

--- stderr
--- exit 0
